--- instruction.md ---
     1	Our minimal images ship without `sed`, and the scripts that still call it are being pointed at `/app/pysed/sed.py` instead. Implement it so that `python3 /app/pysed/sed.py ARGS...` behaves exactly like GNU sed 4.9 run as `sed ARGS...` with `LC_ALL=C`: the same standard output, byte for byte, and the same exit status. What goes to standard error is not compared. The GNU sed 4.9 manual is in `/app/docs/sed.txt`.
     2	
     3	It has to cover this part of GNU sed:
     4	
     5	- the options `-n`, `-E` and `-s`, each given as its own argument before the script, and scripts given with `-e SCRIPT` (repeatable, the pieces joined by newlines) or as the first non-option argument, followed by the names of input files, or none to read standard input;
     6	- from chapter 3 of the manual: `a`, `i` and `c` in their one-line and multi-line forms, `b`, `t`, `T` and `:label`, `{` `}` blocks, `!`, comments and a script whose first two characters are `#n`, `d`, `D`, `g`, `G`, `h`, `H`, `x`, `n`, `N`, `p`, `P`, `q` and `Q` with their exit codes, `=`, `z`, and `s` with the flags `g`, `p` and a number, in any combination. The commands `e`, `F`, `l`, `r`, `R`, `v`, `w`, `W` and `y` and the `s` flags `e`, `i`, `I`, `m`, `M` and `w` are not used;
     7	- every address form in chapter 4, including `first~step`, `addr1,+N`, `addr1,~N` and `0,/regexp/`, with any delimiter for a regular expression; the `I` and `M` modifiers are not used;
     8	- the regular expressions of chapter 5, basic and extended: the operators of sections 5.3 and 5.4, groups (whose text `\1` to `\9` and `&` put into a replacement), bracket expressions with ranges, negation and the `[:class:]` names, the escapes `\w`, `\W`, `\s`, `\S`, `\b`, `\B`, `\<` and `\>`, and the escapes `\a`, `\f`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` and `\v` of section 5.8 wherever they may appear. Back-references inside a regular expression (`\1` in the pattern itself), `` \` ``, `\'`, `\cX`, `\dNNN`, `\oNNN`, `\xHH`, equivalence classes, collating symbols and the case-conversion escapes `\L`, `\U`, `\l`, `\u` and `\E` are not used. Matching is POSIX leftmost-longest: of the matches that start at the leftmost possible position, the longest one wins, whichever alternative of a `|` produces it, whatever the manual's section on `\|` suggests. When several ways of matching give that same text, which parts the groups capture is not checked.
     9	
    10	Scripts never fail and always finish. Input is at most a few dozen lines of ASCII text, each ending with a newline; files may be empty, and a file named on the command line may not exist. Nothing sets `POSIXLY_CORRECT`. Each run has to finish within five seconds. The editing has to be done in Python itself: the program may not start other processes or programs or load native code through `ctypes`, and nothing under `/app` may be a compiled executable or shared library. The checks run it under a Python audit hook that ends it as soon as it tries. Python's standard library, `re` included, is fine, and Python's own `__pycache__` bytecode does not count; only `/app` is carried over to the checks, so packages installed anywhere else are not available.
    11	
    12	It will be checked against GNU sed 4.9 itself with about seven hundred scripts and inputs, from one-line substitutions to multi-line scripts that juggle the hold space, over standard input and several files. Every check relies only on behaviour that the manual describes, together with the rules above. Where GNU sed 4.9 itself departs from its manual, nothing is checked; the known places are a second `*` right after a repetition in a basic regular expression, `^` or `$` in the middle of an extended one, a backslash right after the whitespace of a one-line `a`, `i` or `c`, and a `c` whose range is still open when the input ends.
