   367	
   368	3.1 ‘sed’ script overview
   369	=========================
   370	
   371	A ‘sed’ program consists of one or more ‘sed’ commands, passed in by one
   372	or more of the ‘-e’, ‘-f’, ‘--expression’, and ‘--file’ options, or the
   373	first non-option argument if zero of these options are used.  This
   374	document will refer to “the” ‘sed’ script; this is understood to mean
   375	the in-order concatenation of all of the SCRIPTs and SCRIPT-FILEs passed
   376	in.  *Note Overview::.
   377	
   378	   ‘sed’ commands follow this syntax:
   379	
   380	     [addr]X[options]
   381	
   382	   X is a single-letter ‘sed’ command.  ‘[addr]’ is an optional line
   383	address.  If ‘[addr]’ is specified, the command X will be executed only
   384	on the matched lines.  ‘[addr]’ can be a single line number, a regular
   385	expression, or a range of lines (*note sed addresses::).  Additional
   386	‘[options]’ are used for some ‘sed’ commands.
   387	
   388	   The following example deletes lines 30 to 35 in the input.  ‘30,35’
   389	is an address range.  ‘d’ is the delete command:
   390	
   391	     sed '30,35d' input.txt > output.txt
   392	
   393	   The following example prints all input until a line starting with the
   394	string ‘foo’ is found.  If such line is found, ‘sed’ will terminate with
   395	exit status 42.  If such line was not found (and no other error
   396	occurred), ‘sed’ will exit with status 0.  ‘/^foo/’ is a
   397	regular-expression address.  ‘q’ is the quit command.  ‘42’ is the
   398	command option.
   399	
   400	     sed '/^foo/q42' input.txt > output.txt
   401	
   402	   Commands within a SCRIPT or SCRIPT-FILE can be separated by
   403	semicolons (‘;’) or newlines (ASCII 10).  Multiple scripts can be
   404	specified with ‘-e’ or ‘-f’ options.
   405	
   406	   The following examples are all equivalent.  They perform two ‘sed’
   407	operations: deleting any lines matching the regular expression ‘/^foo/’,
   408	and replacing all occurrences of the string ‘hello’ with ‘world’:
   409	
   410	     sed '/^foo/d ; s/hello/world/g' input.txt > output.txt
   411	
   412	     sed -e '/^foo/d' -e 's/hello/world/g' input.txt > output.txt
   413	
   414	     echo '/^foo/d' > script.sed
   415	     echo 's/hello/world/g' >> script.sed
   416	     sed -f script.sed input.txt > output.txt
   417	
   418	     echo 's/hello/world/g' > script2.sed
   419	     sed -e '/^foo/d' -f script2.sed input.txt > output.txt
   420	
   421	   Commands ‘a’, ‘c’, ‘i’, due to their syntax, cannot be followed by
   422	semicolons working as command separators and thus should be terminated
   423	with newlines or be placed at the end of a SCRIPT or SCRIPT-FILE.
   424	Commands can also be preceded with optional non-significant whitespace
   425	characters.  *Note Multiple commands syntax::.
   426	
   427	3.2 ‘sed’ commands summary
   428	==========================
   429	
   430	The following commands are supported in GNU ‘sed’.  Some are standard
   431	POSIX commands, while other are GNU extensions.  Details and examples
   432	for each command are in the following sections.  (Mnemonics) are shown
   433	in parentheses.
   434	
   435	‘a\’
   436	‘TEXT’
   437	     Append TEXT after a line.
   438	
   439	‘a TEXT’
   440	     Append TEXT after a line (alternative syntax).
   441	
   442	‘b LABEL’
   443	     Branch unconditionally to LABEL.  The LABEL may be omitted, in
   444	     which case the next cycle is started.
   445	
   446	‘c\’
   447	‘TEXT’
   448	     Replace (change) lines with TEXT.
   449	
   450	‘c TEXT’
   451	     Replace (change) lines with TEXT (alternative syntax).
   452	
   453	‘d’
   454	     Delete the pattern space; immediately start next cycle.
   455	
   456	‘D’
   457	     If pattern space contains newlines, delete text in the pattern
   458	     space up to the first newline, and restart cycle with the resultant
   459	     pattern space, without reading a new line of input.
   460	
   461	     If pattern space contains no newline, start a normal new cycle as
   462	     if the ‘d’ command was issued.
   463	
   464	‘e’
   465	     Executes the command that is found in pattern space and replaces
   466	     the pattern space with the output; a trailing newline is
   467	     suppressed.
   468	
   469	‘e COMMAND’
   470	     Executes COMMAND and sends its output to the output stream.  The
   471	     command can run across multiple lines, all but the last ending with
   472	     a back-slash.
   473	
   474	‘F’
   475	     (filename) Print the file name of the current input file (with a
   476	     trailing newline).
   477	
   478	‘g’
   479	     Replace the contents of the pattern space with the contents of the
   480	     hold space.
   481	
   482	‘G’
   483	     Append a newline to the contents of the pattern space, and then
   484	     append the contents of the hold space to that of the pattern space.
   485	
   486	‘h’
   487	     (hold) Replace the contents of the hold space with the contents of
   488	     the pattern space.
   489	
   490	‘H’
   491	     Append a newline to the contents of the hold space, and then append
   492	     the contents of the pattern space to that of the hold space.
   493	
   494	‘i\’
   495	‘TEXT’
   496	     insert TEXT before a line.
   497	
   498	‘i TEXT’
   499	     insert TEXT before a line (alternative syntax).
   500	
   501	‘l’
   502	     Print the pattern space in an unambiguous form.
   503	
   504	‘n’
   505	     (next) If auto-print is not disabled, print the pattern space,
   506	     then, regardless, replace the pattern space with the next line of
   507	     input.  If there is no more input then ‘sed’ exits without
   508	     processing any more commands.
   509	
   510	‘N’
   511	     Add a newline to the pattern space, then append the next line of
   512	     input to the pattern space.  If there is no more input then ‘sed’
   513	     exits without processing any more commands.
   514	
   515	‘p’
   516	     Print the pattern space.
   517	
   518	‘P’
   519	     Print the pattern space, up to the first <newline>.
   520	
   521	‘q[EXIT-CODE]’
   522	     (quit) Exit ‘sed’ without processing any more commands or input.
   523	
   524	‘Q[EXIT-CODE]’
   525	     (quit) This command is the same as ‘q’, but will not print the
   526	     contents of pattern space.  Like ‘q’, it provides the ability to
   527	     return an exit code to the caller.
   528	
   529	‘r filename’
   530	     Reads file FILENAME.
   531	
   532	‘R filename’
   533	     Queue a line of FILENAME to be read and inserted into the output
   534	     stream at the end of the current cycle, or when the next input line
   535	     is read.
   536	
   537	‘s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/[FLAGS]’
   538	     (substitute) Match the regular-expression against the content of
   539	     the pattern space.  If found, replace matched string with
   540	     REPLACEMENT.
   541	
   542	‘t LABEL’
   543	     (test) Branch to LABEL only if there has been a successful
   544	     ‘s’ubstitution since the last input line was read or conditional
   545	     branch was taken.  The LABEL may be omitted, in which case the next
   546	     cycle is started.
   547	
   548	‘T LABEL’
   549	     (test) Branch to LABEL only if there have been no successful
   550	     ‘s’ubstitutions since the last input line was read or conditional
   551	     branch was taken.  The LABEL may be omitted, in which case the next
   552	     cycle is started.
   553	
   554	‘v [VERSION]’
   555	     (version) This command does nothing, but makes ‘sed’ fail if GNU
   556	     ‘sed’ extensions are not supported, or if the requested version is
   557	     not available.
   558	
   559	‘w filename’
   560	     Write the pattern space to FILENAME.
   561	
   562	‘W filename’
   563	     Write to the given filename the portion of the pattern space up to
   564	     the first newline
   565	
   566	‘x’
   567	     Exchange the contents of the hold and pattern spaces.
   568	
   569	‘y/src/dst/’
   570	     Transliterate any characters in the pattern space which match any
   571	     of the SOURCE-CHARS with the corresponding character in DEST-CHARS.
   572	
   573	‘z’
   574	     (zap) This command empties the content of pattern space.
   575	
   576	‘#’
   577	     A comment, until the next newline.
   578	
   579	‘{ CMD ; CMD ... }’
   580	     Group several commands together.
   581	
   582	‘=’
   583	     Print the current input line number (with a trailing newline).
   584	
   585	‘: LABEL’
   586	     Specify the location of LABEL for branch commands (‘b’, ‘t’, ‘T’).
   587	
   588	3.3 The ‘s’ Command
   589	===================
   590	
   591	The ‘s’ command (as in substitute) is probably the most important in
   592	‘sed’ and has a lot of different options.  The syntax of the ‘s’ command
   593	is ‘s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS’.
   594	
   595	   Its basic concept is simple: the ‘s’ command attempts to match the
   596	pattern space against the supplied regular expression REGEXP; if the
   597	match is successful, then that portion of the pattern space which was
   598	matched is replaced with REPLACEMENT.
   599	
   600	   For details about REGEXP syntax *note Regular Expression Addresses:
   601	Regexp Addresses.
   602	
   603	   The REPLACEMENT can contain ‘\N’ (N being a number from 1 to 9,
   604	inclusive) references, which refer to the portion of the match which is
   605	contained between the Nth ‘\(’ and its matching ‘\)’.  Also, the
   606	REPLACEMENT can contain unescaped ‘&’ characters which reference the
   607	whole matched portion of the pattern space.
   608	
   609	   The ‘/’ characters may be uniformly replaced by any other single
   610	character within any given ‘s’ command.  The ‘/’ character (or whatever
   611	other character is used in its stead) can appear in the REGEXP or
   612	REPLACEMENT only if it is preceded by a ‘\’ character.
   613	
   614	   Finally, as a GNU ‘sed’ extension, you can include a special sequence
   615	made of a backslash and one of the letters ‘L’, ‘l’, ‘U’, ‘u’, or ‘E’.
   616	The meaning is as follows:
   617	
   618	‘\L’
   619	     Turn the replacement to lowercase until a ‘\U’ or ‘\E’ is found,
   620	
   621	‘\l’
   622	     Turn the next character to lowercase,
   623	
   624	‘\U’
   625	     Turn the replacement to uppercase until a ‘\L’ or ‘\E’ is found,
   626	
   627	‘\u’
   628	     Turn the next character to uppercase,
   629	
   630	‘\E’
   631	     Stop case conversion started by ‘\L’ or ‘\U’.
   632	
   633	   When the ‘g’ flag is being used, case conversion does not propagate
   634	from one occurrence of the regular expression to another.  For example,
   635	when the following command is executed with ‘a-b-’ in pattern space:
   636	     s/\(b\?\)-/x\u\1/g
   637	
   638	the output is ‘axxB’.  When replacing the first ‘-’, the ‘\u’ sequence
   639	only affects the empty replacement of ‘\1’.  It does not affect the ‘x’
   640	character that is added to pattern space when replacing ‘b-’ with ‘xB’.
   641	
   642	   On the other hand, ‘\l’ and ‘\u’ do affect the remainder of the
   643	replacement text if they are followed by an empty substitution.  With
   644	‘a-b-’ in pattern space, the following command:
   645	     s/\(b\?\)-/\u\1x/g
   646	
   647	will replace ‘-’ with ‘X’ (uppercase) and ‘b-’ with ‘Bx’.  If this
   648	behavior is undesirable, you can prevent it by adding a ‘\E’
   649	sequence—after ‘\1’ in this case.
   650	
   651	   To include a literal ‘\’, ‘&’, or newline in the final replacement,
   652	be sure to precede the desired ‘\’, ‘&’, or newline in the REPLACEMENT
   653	with a ‘\’.
   654	
   655	   The ‘s’ command can be followed by zero or more of the following
   656	FLAGS:
   657	
   658	‘g’
   659	     Apply the replacement to _all_ matches to the REGEXP, not just the
   660	     first.
   661	
   662	‘NUMBER’
   663	     Only replace the NUMBERth match of the REGEXP.
   664	
   665	     interaction in ‘s’ command Note: the POSIX standard does not
   666	     specify what should happen when you mix the ‘g’ and NUMBER
   667	     modifiers, and currently there is no widely agreed upon meaning
   668	     across ‘sed’ implementations.  For GNU ‘sed’, the interaction is
   669	     defined to be: ignore matches before the NUMBERth, and then match
   670	     and replace all matches from the NUMBERth on.
   671	
   672	‘p’
   673	     If the substitution was made, then print the new pattern space.
   674	
   675	     Note: when both the ‘p’ and ‘e’ options are specified, the relative
   676	     ordering of the two produces very different results.  In general,
   677	     ‘ep’ (evaluate then print) is what you want, but operating the
   678	     other way round can be useful for debugging.  For this reason, the
   679	     current version of GNU ‘sed’ interprets specially the presence of
   680	     ‘p’ options both before and after ‘e’, printing the pattern space
   681	     before and after evaluation, while in general flags for the ‘s’
   682	     command show their effect just once.  This behavior, although
   683	     documented, might change in future versions.
   684	
   685	‘w FILENAME’
   686	     If the substitution was made, then write out the result to the
   687	     named file.  As a GNU ‘sed’ extension, two special values of
   688	     FILENAME are supported: ‘/dev/stderr’, which writes the result to
   689	     the standard error, and ‘/dev/stdout’, which writes to the standard
   690	     output.(1)
   691	
   692	‘e’
   693	     This command allows one to pipe input from a shell command into
   694	     pattern space.  If a substitution was made, the command that is
   695	     found in pattern space is executed and pattern space is replaced
   696	     with its output.  A trailing newline is suppressed; results are
   697	     undefined if the command to be executed contains a NUL character.
   698	     This is a GNU ‘sed’ extension.
   699	
   700	‘I’
   701	‘i’
   702	     The ‘I’ modifier to regular-expression matching is a GNU extension
   703	     which makes ‘sed’ match REGEXP in a case-insensitive manner.
   704	
   705	‘M’
   706	‘m’
   707	     The ‘M’ modifier to regular-expression matching is a GNU ‘sed’
   708	     extension which directs GNU ‘sed’ to match the regular expression
   709	     in ‘multi-line’ mode.  The modifier causes ‘^’ and ‘$’ to match
   710	     respectively (in addition to the normal behavior) the empty string
   711	     after a newline, and the empty string before a newline.  There are
   712	     special character sequences (‘\`’ and ‘\'’) which always match the
   713	     beginning or the end of the buffer.  In addition, the period
   714	     character does not match a new-line character in multi-line mode.
   715	
   716	   ---------- Footnotes ----------
   717	
   718	   (1) This is equivalent to ‘p’ unless the ‘-i’ option is being used.
   719	
   720	3.4 Often-Used Commands

--- manual 721-1008 ---
   721	=======================
   722	
   723	If you use ‘sed’ at all, you will quite likely want to know these
   724	commands.
   725	
   726	‘#’
   727	     [No addresses allowed.]
   728	
   729	     The ‘#’ character begins a comment; the comment continues until the
   730	     next newline.
   731	
   732	     If you are concerned about portability, be aware that some
   733	     implementations of ‘sed’ (which are not POSIX conforming) may only
   734	     support a single one-line comment, and then only when the very
   735	     first character of the script is a ‘#’.
   736	
   737	     Warning: if the first two characters of the ‘sed’ script are ‘#n’,
   738	     then the ‘-n’ (no-autoprint) option is forced.  If you want to put
   739	     a comment in the first line of your script and that comment begins
   740	     with the letter ‘n’ and you do not want this behavior, then be sure
   741	     to either use a capital ‘N’, or place at least one space before the
   742	     ‘n’.
   743	
   744	‘q [EXIT-CODE]’
   745	     Exit ‘sed’ without processing any more commands or input.
   746	
   747	     Example: stop after printing the second line:
   748	          $ seq 3 | sed 2q
   749	          1
   750	          2
   751	
   752	     This command accepts only one address.  Note that the current
   753	     pattern space is printed if auto-print is not disabled with the
   754	     ‘-n’ options.  The ability to return an exit code from the ‘sed’
   755	     script is a GNU ‘sed’ extension.
   756	
   757	     See also the GNU ‘sed’ extension ‘Q’ command which quits silently
   758	     without printing the current pattern space.
   759	
   760	‘d’
   761	     Delete the pattern space; immediately start next cycle.
   762	
   763	     Example: delete the second input line:
   764	          $ seq 3 | sed 2d
   765	          1
   766	          3
   767	
   768	‘p’
   769	     Print out the pattern space (to the standard output).  This command
   770	     is usually only used in conjunction with the ‘-n’ command-line
   771	     option.
   772	
   773	     Example: print only the second input line:
   774	          $ seq 3 | sed -n 2p
   775	          2
   776	
   777	‘n’
   778	     If auto-print is not disabled, print the pattern space, then,
   779	     regardless, replace the pattern space with the next line of input.
   780	     If there is no more input then ‘sed’ exits without processing any
   781	     more commands.
   782	
   783	     This command is useful to skip lines (e.g.  process every Nth
   784	     line).
   785	
   786	     Example: perform substitution on every 3rd line (i.e.  two ‘n’
   787	     commands skip two lines):
   788	          $ seq 6 | sed 'n;n;s/./x/'
   789	          1
   790	          2
   791	          x
   792	          4
   793	          5
   794	          x
   795	
   796	     GNU ‘sed’ provides an extension address syntax of FIRST~STEP to
   797	     achieve the same result:
   798	
   799	          $ seq 6 | sed '0~3s/./x/'
   800	          1
   801	          2
   802	          x
   803	          4
   804	          5
   805	          x
   806	
   807	‘{ COMMANDS }’
   808	     A group of commands may be enclosed between ‘{’ and ‘}’ characters.
   809	     This is particularly useful when you want a group of commands to be
   810	     triggered by a single address (or address-range) match.
   811	
   812	     Example: perform substitution then print the second input line:
   813	          $ seq 3 | sed -n '2{s/2/X/ ; p}'
   814	          X
   815	
   816	3.5 Less Frequently-Used Commands
   817	=================================
   818	
   819	Though perhaps less frequently used than those in the previous section,
   820	some very small yet useful ‘sed’ scripts can be built with these
   821	commands.
   822	
   823	‘y/SOURCE-CHARS/DEST-CHARS/’
   824	     Transliterate any characters in the pattern space which match any
   825	     of the SOURCE-CHARS with the corresponding character in DEST-CHARS.
   826	
   827	     Example: transliterate ‘a-j’ into ‘0-9’:
   828	          $ echo hello world | sed 'y/abcdefghij/0123456789/'
   829	          74llo worl3
   830	
   831	     (The ‘/’ characters may be uniformly replaced by any other single
   832	     character within any given ‘y’ command.)
   833	
   834	     Instances of the ‘/’ (or whatever other character is used in its
   835	     stead), ‘\’, or newlines can appear in the SOURCE-CHARS or
   836	     DEST-CHARS lists, provide that each instance is escaped by a ‘\’.
   837	     The SOURCE-CHARS and DEST-CHARS lists _must_ contain the same
   838	     number of characters (after de-escaping).
   839	
   840	     See the ‘tr’ command from GNU coreutils for similar functionality.
   841	
   842	‘a TEXT’
   843	     Appending TEXT after a line.  This is a GNU extension to the
   844	     standard ‘a’ command - see below for details.
   845	
   846	     Example: Add ‘hello’ after the second line:
   847	          $ seq 3 | sed '2a hello'
   848	          1
   849	          2
   850	          hello
   851	          3
   852	
   853	     Leading whitespace after the ‘a’ command is ignored.  The text to
   854	     add is read until the end of the line.
   855	
   856	‘a\’
   857	‘TEXT’
   858	     Appending TEXT after a line.
   859	
   860	     Example: Add ‘hello’ after the second line (⊣ indicates printed
   861	     output lines):
   862	          $ seq 3 | sed '2a\
   863	          hello'
   864	          ⊣1
   865	          ⊣2
   866	          ⊣hello
   867	          ⊣3
   868	
   869	     The ‘a’ command queues the lines of text which follow this command
   870	     (each but the last ending with a ‘\’, which are removed from the
   871	     output) to be output at the end of the current cycle, or when the
   872	     next input line is read.
   873	
   874	     As a GNU extension, this command accepts two addresses.
   875	
   876	     Escape sequences in TEXT are processed, so you should use ‘\\’ in
   877	     TEXT to print a single backslash.
   878	
   879	     The commands resume after the last line without a backslash (‘\’) -
   880	     ‘world’ in the following example:
   881	          $ seq 3 | sed '2a\
   882	          hello\
   883	          world
   884	          3s/./X/'
   885	          ⊣1
   886	          ⊣2
   887	          ⊣hello
   888	          ⊣world
   889	          ⊣X
   890	
   891	     As a GNU extension, the ‘a’ command and TEXT can be separated into
   892	     two ‘-e’ parameters, enabling easier scripting:
   893	          $ seq 3 | sed -e '2a\' -e hello
   894	          1
   895	          2
   896	          hello
   897	          3
   898	
   899	          $ sed -e '2a\' -e "$VAR"
   900	
   901	‘i TEXT’
   902	     insert TEXT before a line.  This is a GNU extension to the standard
   903	     ‘i’ command - see below for details.
   904	
   905	     Example: Insert ‘hello’ before the second line:
   906	          $ seq 3 | sed '2i hello'
   907	          1
   908	          hello
   909	          2
   910	          3
   911	
   912	     Leading whitespace after the ‘i’ command is ignored.  The text to
   913	     add is read until the end of the line.
   914	
   915	‘i\’
   916	‘TEXT’
   917	     Immediately output the lines of text which follow this command.
   918	
   919	     Example: Insert ‘hello’ before the second line (⊣ indicates printed
   920	     output lines):
   921	          $ seq 3 | sed '2i\
   922	          hello'
   923	          ⊣1
   924	          ⊣hello
   925	          ⊣2
   926	          ⊣3
   927	
   928	     As a GNU extension, this command accepts two addresses.
   929	
   930	     Escape sequences in TEXT are processed, so you should use ‘\\’ in
   931	     TEXT to print a single backslash.
   932	
   933	     The commands resume after the last line without a backslash (‘\’) -
   934	     ‘world’ in the following example:
   935	          $ seq 3 | sed '2i\
   936	          hello\
   937	          world
   938	          s/./X/'
   939	          ⊣X
   940	          ⊣hello
   941	          ⊣world
   942	          ⊣X
   943	          ⊣X
   944	
   945	     As a GNU extension, the ‘i’ command and TEXT can be separated into
