   368	3.1 ‘sed’ script overview
   369	=========================
   370	
   371	A ‘sed’ program consists of one or more ‘sed’ commands, passed in by one
   372	or more of the ‘-e’, ‘-f’, ‘--expression’, and ‘--file’ options, or the
   373	first non-option argument if zero of these options are used.  This
   374	document will refer to “the” ‘sed’ script; this is understood to mean
   375	the in-order concatenation of all of the SCRIPTs and SCRIPT-FILEs passed
   376	in.  *Note Overview::.
   377	
   378	   ‘sed’ commands follow this syntax:
   379	
   380	     [addr]X[options]
   381	
   382	   X is a single-letter ‘sed’ command.  ‘[addr]’ is an optional line
   383	address.  If ‘[addr]’ is specified, the command X will be executed only
   384	on the matched lines.  ‘[addr]’ can be a single line number, a regular
   385	expression, or a range of lines (*note sed addresses::).  Additional
   386	‘[options]’ are used for some ‘sed’ commands.
   387	
   388	   The following example deletes lines 30 to 35 in the input.  ‘30,35’
   389	is an address range.  ‘d’ is the delete command:
   390	
   391	     sed '30,35d' input.txt > output.txt
   392	
   393	   The following example prints all input until a line starting with the
   394	string ‘foo’ is found.  If such line is found, ‘sed’ will terminate with
   395	exit status 42.  If such line was not found (and no other error
   396	occurred), ‘sed’ will exit with status 0.  ‘/^foo/’ is a
   397	regular-expression address.  ‘q’ is the quit command.  ‘42’ is the
   398	command option.
   399	
   400	     sed '/^foo/q42' input.txt > output.txt
   401	
   402	   Commands within a SCRIPT or SCRIPT-FILE can be separated by
   403	semicolons (‘;’) or newlines (ASCII 10).  Multiple scripts can be
   404	specified with ‘-e’ or ‘-f’ options.
   405	
   406	   The following examples are all equivalent.  They perform two ‘sed’
   407	operations: deleting any lines matching the regular expression ‘/^foo/’,
   408	and replacing all occurrences of the string ‘hello’ with ‘world’:
   409	
   410	     sed '/^foo/d ; s/hello/world/g' input.txt > output.txt
   411	
   412	     sed -e '/^foo/d' -e 's/hello/world/g' input.txt > output.txt
   413	
   414	     echo '/^foo/d' > script.sed
   415	     echo 's/hello/world/g' >> script.sed
   416	     sed -f script.sed input.txt > output.txt
   417	
   418	     echo 's/hello/world/g' > script2.sed
   419	     sed -e '/^foo/d' -f script2.sed input.txt > output.txt
   420	
   421	   Commands ‘a’, ‘c’, ‘i’, due to their syntax, cannot be followed by
   422	semicolons working as command separators and thus should be terminated
   423	with newlines or be placed at the end of a SCRIPT or SCRIPT-FILE.
   424	Commands can also be preceded with optional non-significant whitespace
   425	characters.  *Note Multiple commands syntax::.
