   427	3.2 ‘sed’ commands summary
   428	==========================
   429	
   430	The following commands are supported in GNU ‘sed’.  Some are standard
   431	POSIX commands, while other are GNU extensions.  Details and examples
   432	for each command are in the following sections.  (Mnemonics) are shown
   433	in parentheses.
   434	
   435	‘a\’
   436	‘TEXT’
   437	     Append TEXT after a line.
   438	
   439	‘a TEXT’
   440	     Append TEXT after a line (alternative syntax).
   441	
   442	‘b LABEL’
   443	     Branch unconditionally to LABEL.  The LABEL may be omitted, in
   444	     which case the next cycle is started.
   445	
   446	‘c\’
   447	‘TEXT’
   448	     Replace (change) lines with TEXT.
   449	
   450	‘c TEXT’
   451	     Replace (change) lines with TEXT (alternative syntax).
   452	
   453	‘d’
   454	     Delete the pattern space; immediately start next cycle.
   455	
   456	‘D’
   457	     If pattern space contains newlines, delete text in the pattern
   458	     space up to the first newline, and restart cycle with the resultant
   459	     pattern space, without reading a new line of input.
   460	
   461	     If pattern space contains no newline, start a normal new cycle as
   462	     if the ‘d’ command was issued.
   463	
   464	‘e’
   465	     Executes the command that is found in pattern space and replaces
   466	     the pattern space with the output; a trailing newline is
   467	     suppressed.
   468	
   469	‘e COMMAND’
   470	     Executes COMMAND and sends its output to the output stream.  The
   471	     command can run across multiple lines, all but the last ending with
   472	     a back-slash.
   473	
   474	‘F’
   475	     (filename) Print the file name of the current input file (with a
   476	     trailing newline).
   477	
   478	‘g’
   479	     Replace the contents of the pattern space with the contents of the
   480	     hold space.
   481	
   482	‘G’
   483	     Append a newline to the contents of the pattern space, and then
   484	     append the contents of the hold space to that of the pattern space.
   485	
   486	‘h’
   487	     (hold) Replace the contents of the hold space with the contents of
   488	     the pattern space.
   489	
   490	‘H’
   491	     Append a newline to the contents of the hold space, and then append
   492	     the contents of the pattern space to that of the hold space.
   493	
   494	‘i\’
   495	‘TEXT’
   496	     insert TEXT before a line.
   497	
   498	‘i TEXT’
   499	     insert TEXT before a line (alternative syntax).
   500	
   501	‘l’
   502	     Print the pattern space in an unambiguous form.
   503	
   504	‘n’
   505	     (next) If auto-print is not disabled, print the pattern space,
   506	     then, regardless, replace the pattern space with the next line of
   507	     input.  If there is no more input then ‘sed’ exits without
   508	     processing any more commands.
   509	
   510	‘N’
   511	     Add a newline to the pattern space, then append the next line of
   512	     input to the pattern space.  If there is no more input then ‘sed’
   513	     exits without processing any more commands.
   514	
   515	‘p’
   516	     Print the pattern space.
   517	
   518	‘P’
   519	     Print the pattern space, up to the first <newline>.
   520	
   521	‘q[EXIT-CODE]’
   522	     (quit) Exit ‘sed’ without processing any more commands or input.
   523	
   524	‘Q[EXIT-CODE]’
   525	     (quit) This command is the same as ‘q’, but will not print the
   526	     contents of pattern space.  Like ‘q’, it provides the ability to
   527	     return an exit code to the caller.
   528	
   529	‘r filename’
   530	     Reads file FILENAME.
   531	
   532	‘R filename’
   533	     Queue a line of FILENAME to be read and inserted into the output
   534	     stream at the end of the current cycle, or when the next input line
   535	     is read.
   536	
   537	‘s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/[FLAGS]’
   538	     (substitute) Match the regular-expression against the content of
   539	     the pattern space.  If found, replace matched string with
   540	     REPLACEMENT.
   541	
   542	‘t LABEL’
   543	     (test) Branch to LABEL only if there has been a successful
   544	     ‘s’ubstitution since the last input line was read or conditional
   545	     branch was taken.  The LABEL may be omitted, in which case the next
   546	     cycle is started.
   547	
   548	‘T LABEL’
   549	     (test) Branch to LABEL only if there have been no successful
   550	     ‘s’ubstitutions since the last input line was read or conditional
   551	     branch was taken.  The LABEL may be omitted, in which case the next
   552	     cycle is started.
