   720	3.4 Often-Used Commands

--- 720-900 ---
   720	3.4 Often-Used Commands
   721	=======================
   722	
   723	If you use ‘sed’ at all, you will quite likely want to know these
   724	commands.
   725	
   726	‘#’
   727	     [No addresses allowed.]
   728	
   729	     The ‘#’ character begins a comment; the comment continues until the
   730	     next newline.
   731	
   732	     If you are concerned about portability, be aware that some
   733	     implementations of ‘sed’ (which are not POSIX conforming) may only
   734	     support a single one-line comment, and then only when the very
   735	     first character of the script is a ‘#’.
   736	
   737	     Warning: if the first two characters of the ‘sed’ script are ‘#n’,
   738	     then the ‘-n’ (no-autoprint) option is forced.  If you want to put
   739	     a comment in the first line of your script and that comment begins
   740	     with the letter ‘n’ and you do not want this behavior, then be sure
   741	     to either use a capital ‘N’, or place at least one space before the
   742	     ‘n’.
   743	
   744	‘q [EXIT-CODE]’
   745	     Exit ‘sed’ without processing any more commands or input.
   746	
   747	     Example: stop after printing the second line:
   748	          $ seq 3 | sed 2q
   749	          1
   750	          2
   751	
   752	     This command accepts only one address.  Note that the current
   753	     pattern space is printed if auto-print is not disabled with the
   754	     ‘-n’ options.  The ability to return an exit code from the ‘sed’
   755	     script is a GNU ‘sed’ extension.
   756	
   757	     See also the GNU ‘sed’ extension ‘Q’ command which quits silently
   758	     without printing the current pattern space.
   759	
   760	‘d’
   761	     Delete the pattern space; immediately start next cycle.
   762	
   763	     Example: delete the second input line:
   764	          $ seq 3 | sed 2d
   765	          1
   766	          3
   767	
   768	‘p’
