  1111	3.6 Commands for ‘sed’ gurus
  1112	============================
  1113	
  1114	In most cases, use of these commands indicates that you are probably
  1115	better off programming in something like ‘awk’ or Perl.  But

--- 1111-1282 ---
  1111	3.6 Commands for ‘sed’ gurus
  1112	============================
  1113	
  1114	In most cases, use of these commands indicates that you are probably
  1115	better off programming in something like ‘awk’ or Perl.  But
  1116	occasionally one is committed to sticking with ‘sed’, and these commands
  1117	can enable one to write quite convoluted scripts.
  1118	
  1119	‘: LABEL’
  1120	     [No addresses allowed.]
  1121	
  1122	     Specify the location of LABEL for branch commands.  In all other
  1123	     respects, a no-op.
  1124	
  1125	‘b LABEL’
  1126	     Unconditionally branch to LABEL.  The LABEL may be omitted, in
  1127	     which case the next cycle is started.
  1128	
  1129	‘t LABEL’
  1130	     Branch to LABEL only if there has been a successful ‘s’ubstitution
  1131	     since the last input line was read or conditional branch was taken.
  1132	     The LABEL may be omitted, in which case the next cycle is started.
  1133	
  1134	3.7 Commands Specific to GNU ‘sed’
  1135	==================================
  1136	
  1137	These commands are specific to GNU ‘sed’, so you must use them with care
  1138	and only when you are sure that hindering portability is not evil.  They
  1139	allow you to check for GNU ‘sed’ extensions or to do tasks that are
  1140	required quite often, yet are unsupported by standard ‘sed’s.
  1141	
  1142	‘e [COMMAND]’
  1143	     This command allows one to pipe input from a shell command into
  1144	     pattern space.  Without parameters, the ‘e’ command executes the
  1145	     command that is found in pattern space and replaces the pattern
  1146	     space with the output; a trailing newline is suppressed.
  1147	
  1148	     If a parameter is specified, instead, the ‘e’ command interprets it
  1149	     as a command and sends its output to the output stream.  The
  1150	     command can run across multiple lines, all but the last ending with
  1151	     a back-slash.
  1152	
  1153	     In both cases, the results are undefined if the command to be
  1154	     executed contains a NUL character.
  1155	
  1156	     Note that, unlike the ‘r’ command, the output of the command will
  1157	     be printed immediately; the ‘r’ command instead delays the output
  1158	     to the end of the current cycle.
  1159	
  1160	‘F’
  1161	     Print out the file name of the current input file (with a trailing
  1162	     newline).
  1163	
  1164	‘Q [EXIT-CODE]’
  1165	     This command accepts only one address.
  1166	
  1167	     This command is the same as ‘q’, but will not print the contents of
  1168	     pattern space.  Like ‘q’, it provides the ability to return an exit
  1169	     code to the caller.
  1170	
  1171	     This command can be useful because the only alternative ways to
  1172	     accomplish this apparently trivial function are to use the ‘-n’
  1173	     option (which can unnecessarily complicate your script) or
  1174	     resorting to the following snippet, which wastes time by reading
  1175	     the whole file without any visible effect:
  1176	
  1177	          :eat
  1178	          $d       Quit silently on the last line
  1179	          N        Read another line, silently
  1180	          g        Overwrite pattern space each time to save memory
  1181	          b eat
  1182	
  1183	‘R FILENAME’
  1184	     Queue a line of FILENAME to be read and inserted into the output
  1185	     stream at the end of the current cycle, or when the next input line
  1186	     is read.  Note that if FILENAME cannot be read, or if its end is
  1187	     reached, no line is appended, without any error indication.
  1188	
  1189	     As with the ‘r’ command, the special value ‘/dev/stdin’ is
  1190	     supported for the file name, which reads a line from the standard
  1191	     input.
  1192	
  1193	‘T LABEL’
  1194	     Branch to LABEL only if there have been no successful
  1195	     ‘s’ubstitutions since the last input line was read or conditional
  1196	     branch was taken.  The LABEL may be omitted, in which case the next
  1197	     cycle is started.
  1198	
  1199	‘v VERSION’
  1200	     This command does nothing, but makes ‘sed’ fail if GNU ‘sed’
  1201	     extensions are not supported, simply because other versions of
  1202	     ‘sed’ do not implement it.  In addition, you can specify the
  1203	     version of ‘sed’ that your script requires, such as ‘4.0.5’.  The
  1204	     default is ‘4.0’ because that is the first version that implemented
  1205	     this command.
  1206	
  1207	     This command enables all GNU extensions even if ‘POSIXLY_CORRECT’
  1208	     is set in the environment.
  1209	
  1210	‘W FILENAME’
  1211	     Write to the given filename the portion of the pattern space up to
  1212	     the first newline.  Everything said under the ‘w’ command about
  1213	     file handling holds here too.
  1214	
  1215	‘z’
  1216	     This command empties the content of pattern space.  It is usually
  1217	     the same as ‘s/.*//’, but is more efficient and works in the
  1218	     presence of invalid multibyte sequences in the input stream.  POSIX
  1219	     mandates that such sequences are _not_ matched by ‘.’, so that
  1220	     there is no portable way to clear ‘sed’’s buffers in the middle of
  1221	     the script in most multibyte locales (including UTF-8 locales).
  1222	
  1223	3.8 Multiple commands syntax
  1224	============================
  1225	
  1226	There are several methods to specify multiple commands in a ‘sed’
  1227	program.
  1228	
  1229	   Using newlines is most natural when running a sed script from a file
  1230	(using the ‘-f’ option).
  1231	
  1232	   On the command line, all ‘sed’ commands may be separated by newlines.
  1233	Alternatively, you may specify each command as an argument to an ‘-e’
  1234	option:
  1235	
  1236	     $ seq 6 | sed '1d
  1237	     3d
  1238	     5d'
  1239	     2
  1240	     4
  1241	     6
  1242	
  1243	     $ seq 6 | sed -e 1d -e 3d -e 5d
  1244	     2
  1245	     4
  1246	     6
  1247	
  1248	   A semicolon (‘;’) may be used to separate most simple commands:
  1249	
  1250	     $ seq 6 | sed '1d;3d;5d'
  1251	     2
  1252	     4
  1253	     6
  1254	
  1255	   The ‘{’,‘}’,‘b’,‘t’,‘T’,‘:’ commands can be separated with a
  1256	semicolon (this is a non-portable GNU ‘sed’ extension).
  1257	
  1258	     $ seq 4 | sed '{1d;3d}'
  1259	     2
  1260	     4
  1261	
  1262	     $ seq 6 | sed '{1d;3d};5d'
  1263	     2
  1264	     4
  1265	     6
  1266	
  1267	   Labels used in ‘b’,‘t’,‘T’,‘:’ commands are read until a semicolon.
  1268	Leading and trailing whitespace is ignored.  In the examples below the
  1269	label is ‘x’.  The first example works with GNU ‘sed’.  The second is a
  1270	portable equivalent.  For more information about branching and labels
  1271	*note Branching and flow control::.
  1272	
  1273	     $ seq 3 | sed '/1/b x ; s/^/=/ ; :x ; 3d'
  1274	     1
  1275	     =2
  1276	
  1277	     $ seq 3 | sed -e '/1/bx' -e 's/^/=/' -e ':x' -e '3d'
  1278	     1
  1279	     =2
  1280	
