   588	3.3 The ‘s’ Command
   589	===================
   590	
   591	The ‘s’ command (as in substitute) is probably the most important in
   592	‘sed’ and has a lot of different options.  The syntax of the ‘s’ command
   593	is ‘s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS’.
   594	
   595	   Its basic concept is simple: the ‘s’ command attempts to match the
   596	pattern space against the supplied regular expression REGEXP; if the
   597	match is successful, then that portion of the pattern space which was
   598	matched is replaced with REPLACEMENT.
   599	
   600	   For details about REGEXP syntax *note Regular Expression Addresses:
   601	Regexp Addresses.
   602	
   603	   The REPLACEMENT can contain ‘\N’ (N being a number from 1 to 9,
   604	inclusive) references, which refer to the portion of the match which is
   605	contained between the Nth ‘\(’ and its matching ‘\)’.  Also, the
   606	REPLACEMENT can contain unescaped ‘&’ characters which reference the
   607	whole matched portion of the pattern space.
   608	
   609	   The ‘/’ characters may be uniformly replaced by any other single
   610	character within any given ‘s’ command.  The ‘/’ character (or whatever
   611	other character is used in its stead) can appear in the REGEXP or
   612	REPLACEMENT only if it is preceded by a ‘\’ character.
   613	
   614	   Finally, as a GNU ‘sed’ extension, you can include a special sequence
   615	made of a backslash and one of the letters ‘L’, ‘l’, ‘U’, ‘u’, or ‘E’.
   616	The meaning is as follows:
   617	
   618	‘\L’
   619	     Turn the replacement to lowercase until a ‘\U’ or ‘\E’ is found,
   620	
   621	‘\l’
   622	     Turn the next character to lowercase,
   623	
   624	‘\U’
   625	     Turn the replacement to uppercase until a ‘\L’ or ‘\E’ is found,
   626	
   627	‘\u’
   628	     Turn the next character to uppercase,
   629	
   630	‘\E’
   631	     Stop case conversion started by ‘\L’ or ‘\U’.
   632	
   633	   When the ‘g’ flag is being used, case conversion does not propagate
   634	from one occurrence of the regular expression to another.  For example,
   635	when the following command is executed with ‘a-b-’ in pattern space:
   636	     s/\(b\?\)-/x\u\1/g
   637	
   638	the output is ‘axxB’.  When replacing the first ‘-’, the ‘\u’ sequence
   639	only affects the empty replacement of ‘\1’.  It does not affect the ‘x’
   640	character that is added to pattern space when replacing ‘b-’ with ‘xB’.
   641	
   642	   On the other hand, ‘\l’ and ‘\u’ do affect the remainder of the
   643	replacement text if they are followed by an empty substitution.  With
   644	‘a-b-’ in pattern space, the following command:
   645	     s/\(b\?\)-/\u\1x/g
   646	
   647	will replace ‘-’ with ‘X’ (uppercase) and ‘b-’ with ‘Bx’.  If this
   648	behavior is undesirable, you can prevent it by adding a ‘\E’
   649	sequence—after ‘\1’ in this case.
   650	
   651	   To include a literal ‘\’, ‘&’, or newline in the final replacement,
   652	be sure to precede the desired ‘\’, ‘&’, or newline in the REPLACEMENT
   653	with a ‘\’.
   654	
   655	   The ‘s’ command can be followed by zero or more of the following
   656	FLAGS:
   657	
   658	‘g’
   659	     Apply the replacement to _all_ matches to the REGEXP, not just the
   660	     first.
   661	
   662	‘NUMBER’
   663	     Only replace the NUMBERth match of the REGEXP.
   664	
   665	     interaction in ‘s’ command Note: the POSIX standard does not
   666	     specify what should happen when you mix the ‘g’ and NUMBER
   667	     modifiers, and currently there is no widely agreed upon meaning
   668	     across ‘sed’ implementations.  For GNU ‘sed’, the interaction is
   669	     defined to be: ignore matches before the NUMBERth, and then match
   670	     and replace all matches from the NUMBERth on.
   671	
   672	‘p’
   673	     If the substitution was made, then print the new pattern space.
   674	
   675	     Note: when both the ‘p’ and ‘e’ options are specified, the relative
   676	     ordering of the two produces very different results.  In general,
   677	     ‘ep’ (evaluate then print) is what you want, but operating the
   678	     other way round can be useful for debugging.  For this reason, the
   679	     current version of GNU ‘sed’ interprets specially the presence of
   680	     ‘p’ options both before and after ‘e’, printing the pattern space
   681	     before and after evaluation, while in general flags for the ‘s’
   682	     command show their effect just once.  This behavior, although
   683	     documented, might change in future versions.
   684	
   685	‘w FILENAME’
   686	     If the substitution was made, then write out the result to the
   687	     named file.  As a GNU ‘sed’ extension, two special values of
   688	     FILENAME are supported: ‘/dev/stderr’, which writes the result to
   689	     the standard error, and ‘/dev/stdout’, which writes to the standard
   690	     output.(1)
   691	
   692	‘e’
   693	     This command allows one to pipe input from a shell command into
   694	     pattern space.  If a substitution was made, the command that is
   695	     found in pattern space is executed and pattern space is replaced
   696	     with its output.  A trailing newline is suppressed; results are
   697	     undefined if the command to be executed contains a NUL character.
   698	     This is a GNU ‘sed’ extension.
   699	
   700	‘I’
   701	‘i’
   702	     The ‘I’ modifier to regular-expression matching is a GNU extension
   703	     which makes ‘sed’ match REGEXP in a case-insensitive manner.
   704	
   705	‘M’
   706	‘m’
   707	     The ‘M’ modifier to regular-expression matching is a GNU ‘sed’
   708	     extension which directs GNU ‘sed’ to match the regular expression
   709	     in ‘multi-line’ mode.  The modifier causes ‘^’ and ‘$’ to match
   710	     respectively (in addition to the normal behavior) the empty string
   711	     after a newline, and the empty string before a newline.  There are
   712	     special character sequences (‘\`’ and ‘\'’) which always match the
   713	     beginning or the end of the buffer.  In addition, the period
   714	     character does not match a new-line character in multi-line mode.
   715	
