   720	3.4 Often-Used Commands
   721	=======================
   722	
   723	If you use ‘sed’ at all, you will quite likely want to know these
   724	commands.
   725	
   726	‘#’
   727	     [No addresses allowed.]
   728	
   729	     The ‘#’ character begins a comment; the comment continues until the
   730	     next newline.
   731	
   732	     If you are concerned about portability, be aware that some
   733	     implementations of ‘sed’ (which are not POSIX conforming) may only
   734	     support a single one-line comment, and then only when the very
   735	     first character of the script is a ‘#’.
   736	
   737	     Warning: if the first two characters of the ‘sed’ script are ‘#n’,
   738	     then the ‘-n’ (no-autoprint) option is forced.  If you want to put
   739	     a comment in the first line of your script and that comment begins
   740	     with the letter ‘n’ and you do not want this behavior, then be sure
   741	     to either use a capital ‘N’, or place at least one space before the
   742	     ‘n’.
   743	
   744	‘q [EXIT-CODE]’
   745	     Exit ‘sed’ without processing any more commands or input.
   746	
   747	     Example: stop after printing the second line:
   748	          $ seq 3 | sed 2q
   749	          1
   750	          2
   751	
   752	     This command accepts only one address.  Note that the current
   753	     pattern space is printed if auto-print is not disabled with the
   754	     ‘-n’ options.  The ability to return an exit code from the ‘sed’
   755	     script is a GNU ‘sed’ extension.
   756	
   757	     See also the GNU ‘sed’ extension ‘Q’ command which quits silently
   758	     without printing the current pattern space.
   759	
   760	‘d’
   761	     Delete the pattern space; immediately start next cycle.
   762	
   763	     Example: delete the second input line:
   764	          $ seq 3 | sed 2d
   765	          1
   766	          3
   767	
   768	‘p’
   769	     Print out the pattern space (to the standard output).  This command
   770	     is usually only used in conjunction with the ‘-n’ command-line
   771	     option.
   772	
   773	     Example: print only the second input line:
   774	          $ seq 3 | sed -n 2p
   775	          2
   776	
   777	‘n’
   778	     If auto-print is not disabled, print the pattern space, then,
   779	     regardless, replace the pattern space with the next line of input.
   780	     If there is no more input then ‘sed’ exits without processing any
   781	     more commands.
   782	
   783	     This command is useful to skip lines (e.g.  process every Nth
   784	     line).
   785	
   786	     Example: perform substitution on every 3rd line (i.e.  two ‘n’
   787	     commands skip two lines):
   788	          $ seq 6 | sed 'n;n;s/./x/'
   789	          1
   790	          2
   791	          x
   792	          4
   793	          5
   794	          x
   795	
   796	     GNU ‘sed’ provides an extension address syntax of FIRST~STEP to
   797	     achieve the same result:
   798	
   799	          $ seq 6 | sed '0~3s/./x/'
   800	          1
   801	          2
   802	          x
   803	          4
   804	          5
   805	          x
   806	
   807	‘{ COMMANDS }’
   808	     A group of commands may be enclosed between ‘{’ and ‘}’ characters.
   809	     This is particularly useful when you want a group of commands to be
   810	     triggered by a single address (or address-range) match.
   811	
   812	     Example: perform substitution then print the second input line:
   813	          $ seq 3 | sed -n '2{s/2/X/ ; p}'
   814	          X
   815	
   816	3.5 Less Frequently-Used Commands
   817	=================================
   818	
   819	Though perhaps less frequently used than those in the previous section,
   820	some very small yet useful ‘sed’ scripts can be built with these
   821	commands.
   822	
   823	‘y/SOURCE-CHARS/DEST-CHARS/’
   824	     Transliterate any characters in the pattern space which match any
   825	     of the SOURCE-CHARS with the corresponding character in DEST-CHARS.
   826	
   827	     Example: transliterate ‘a-j’ into ‘0-9’:
   828	          $ echo hello world | sed 'y/abcdefghij/0123456789/'
   829	          74llo worl3
   830	
   831	     (The ‘/’ characters may be uniformly replaced by any other single
   832	     character within any given ‘y’ command.)
   833	
   834	     Instances of the ‘/’ (or whatever other character is used in its
   835	     stead), ‘\’, or newlines can appear in the SOURCE-CHARS or
   836	     DEST-CHARS lists, provide that each instance is escaped by a ‘\’.
   837	     The SOURCE-CHARS and DEST-CHARS lists _must_ contain the same
   838	     number of characters (after de-escaping).
   839	
   840	     See the ‘tr’ command from GNU coreutils for similar functionality.
   841	
   842	‘a TEXT’
   843	     Appending TEXT after a line.  This is a GNU extension to the
   844	     standard ‘a’ command - see below for details.
   845	
   846	     Example: Add ‘hello’ after the second line:
   847	          $ seq 3 | sed '2a hello'
   848	          1
   849	          2
   850	          hello
   851	          3
   852	
   853	     Leading whitespace after the ‘a’ command is ignored.  The text to
   854	     add is read until the end of the line.
   855	
   856	‘a\’
   857	‘TEXT’
   858	     Appending TEXT after a line.
   859	
   860	     Example: Add ‘hello’ after the second line (⊣ indicates printed
   861	     output lines):
   862	          $ seq 3 | sed '2a\
   863	          hello'
   864	          ⊣1
   865	          ⊣2
   866	          ⊣hello
   867	          ⊣3
   868	
   869	     The ‘a’ command queues the lines of text which follow this command
   870	     (each but the last ending with a ‘\’, which are removed from the
   871	     output) to be output at the end of the current cycle, or when the
   872	     next input line is read.
   873	
   874	     As a GNU extension, this command accepts two addresses.
   875	

