2503-   ‘sed’ operates by performing the following cycle on each line of
2504-input: first, ‘sed’ reads one line from the input stream, removes any
2505:trailing newline, and places it in the pattern space.  Then commands are
2506-executed; each command can have an address associated to it: addresses
2507-are a kind of condition code, and a command is only executed if the
2508-condition is verified before the command is to be executed.
2509-
2510-   When the end of the script is reached, unless the ‘-n’ option is in
2511:use, the contents of pattern space are printed out to the output stream,
2512-adding back the trailing newline if it was removed.(1)  Then the next
2513-cycle starts for the next input line.
2514-
2515:   Unless special commands (like ‘D’) are used, the pattern space is
2516:deleted between two cycles.  The hold space, on the other hand, keeps
2517-its data between cycles (see commands ‘h’, ‘H’, ‘x’, ‘g’, ‘G’ to move
2518-data between both buffers).
