2537-‘D’,‘G’,‘H’,‘N’,‘P’.  They are similar to their lowercase counterparts
2538-(‘d’,‘g’, ‘h’,‘n’,‘p’), except that these commands append or subtract
2539-data while respecting embedded newlines - allowing adding and removing
2540:lines from the pattern and hold spaces.
2541-
2542-   They operate as follows:
2543-‘D’
2544:     _deletes_ line from the pattern space until the first newline, and
2545-     restarts the cycle.
2546-
2547-‘G’
2548:     _appends_ line from the hold space to the pattern space, with a
2549-     newline before it.
2550-
2551-‘H’
2552:     _appends_ line from the pattern space to the hold space, with a
2553-     newline before it.
2554-
2555-‘N’
2556:     _appends_ line from the input file to the pattern space.
2557-
2558-‘P’
2559:     _prints_ line from the pattern space until the first newline.
2560-
2561-   The following example illustrates the operation of ‘N’ and ‘D’
2562-commands:
--
2568-     4\n5$
2569-     5\n6$
2570-
2571:  1. ‘sed’ starts by reading the first line into the pattern space (i.e.
2572-     ‘1’).
2573-  2. At the beginning of every cycle, the ‘N’ command appends a newline
2574:     and the next line to the pattern space (i.e.  ‘1’, ‘\n’, ‘2’ in the
2575-     first cycle).
2576:  3. The ‘l’ command prints the content of the pattern space
2577-     unambiguously.
2578:  4. The ‘D’ command then removes the content of pattern space up to the
2579-     first newline (leaving ‘2’ at the end of the first cycle).
2580-  5. At the next cycle the ‘N’ command appends a newline and the next
2581:     input line to the pattern space (e.g.  ‘2’, ‘\n’, ‘3’).
2582-
2583-   A common technique to process blocks of text such as paragraphs
2584-(instead of line-by-line) is using the following construct:
--
2586-     sed '/./{H;$!d} ; x ; s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/'
2587-
2588-  1. The first expression, ‘/./{H;$!d}’ operates on all non-empty lines,
2589:     and adds the current line (in the pattern space) to the hold space.
2590:     On all lines except the last, the pattern space is deleted and the
2591-     cycle is restarted.
2592-
2593-  2. The other expressions ‘x’ and ‘s’ are executed only on empty lines
2594-     (i.e.  paragraph separators).  The ‘x’ command fetches the
2595:     accumulated lines from the hold space back to the pattern space.
2596-     The ‘s///’ command then operates on all the text in the paragraph
2597-     (including the embedded newlines).
